    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Collagen supplements are well tolerated by most people, and the side effects recorded in trials are mild and digestive: fullness, bloating, loose stools.</strong> The real cautions are narrower than the internet suggests. Fish allergy, a history of calcium oxalate kidney stones, pregnancy, and advanced kidney disease are the four situations where you should ask a doctor before starting.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Commonest complaint:</strong> digestive heaviness or bloating in the first week, usually settling.</li>
        <li><strong>Fish allergy is a real contraindication:</strong> 21 of 101 fish-allergic patients had IgE antibodies to fish collagen itself.</li>
        <li><strong>Kidney stones:</strong> the concern is hydroxyproline turning into oxalate. One 30g gelatin dose raised urinary oxalate 43%; a daily shot carries roughly a quarter of that hydroxyproline.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> the evidence calling collagen "well tolerated" is rated very low quality, and no trial has run longer than about a year.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

