      <h2 id="real-effects">What are the real side effects of collagen?</h2>
      <p>Digestive, mild, and usually in the first week. Across trials the reported complaints are a feeling of fullness or heaviness after the dose, bloating, and a change in bowel habit. Rash and loose stools turn up less often. None of this is specific to collagen; it is what any concentrated protein does to some people on an empty stomach.</p>
      <p>What matters more is how confidently anyone can say that. A 2023 meta-analysis in the <em>Journal of Orthopaedic Surgery and Research</em> pooled four knee osteoarthritis trials covering 507 patients and found no statistically significant difference in adverse events between collagen peptides and placebo: odds ratio 1.66, confidence interval 0.99 to 2.78. That interval touches 1.00, the authors rated the certainty as very low, and all four trials were judged at high risk of bias. A larger trial sequential meta-analysis in <em>Osteoarthritis and Cartilage</em> in 2024, covering 35 trials, likewise found no increase in withdrawals or adverse events.</p>
      <p>So the fair summary is not "collagen has no side effects". It is that nobody has found a signal, in studies mostly too small and too short to find a subtle one, and the longest ran about a year.</p>

