      <h2 id="kidney-stones">Can collagen cause kidney stones?</h2>
      <p>For most people, no. For someone who already forms calcium oxalate stones, it is a question worth asking a urologist, and the reason is a single amino acid.</p>
      <p>Collagen is unusually rich in hydroxyproline, and the body converts a fraction of it to oxalate. In a controlled study in <em>Kidney International</em> in 2006, healthy volunteers on a low-oxalate diet ate 30g of gelatin daily, supplying 2,750mg of hydroxyproline. Urinary glycolate rose 5.3-fold and urinary oxalate rose 43%. The authors calculated that only about 0.5% of the hydroxyproline metabolised ended up as urinary oxalate, which is why the oxalate rise was modest next to the glycolate one.</p>
      <p>The dose matters, and here is the arithmetic on our own label rather than a reassurance. Using that study's own ratio, collagen is roughly 9% hydroxyproline by weight. Collabloom's 8,000mg of marine collagen therefore carries around 740mg of hydroxyproline, and <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">Skelecoll</a>'s 10,000mg carries around 920mg. Both sit below 1,000mg. In the one dose-ranging trial, published in <em>Urolithiasis</em> in 2015, 40 healthy adults took 0, 500, 1,000 or 2,000mg of L-hydroxyproline daily for 12 weeks: the 500 and 1,000mg groups showed no change in urinary oxalate, and only the 2,000mg group did, without exceeding levels already seen in stone formers. A smaller 2019 study in <em>International Urology and Nephrology</em> found glycolate and urinary pH rose on hydroxyproline while oxalate did not shift at all.</p>
      <p>That is a calculation from a published hydroxyproline fraction, not a measurement of our batches, and it is not a clean answer: the two human dose-ranging results disagree at the margins, and neither followed anyone long enough to count stones. If you have passed a stone, take the label to your doctor before you take the shot.</p>

      <h2 id="who-should-avoid">Who should not take collagen?</h2>
      <p>Four groups, and only one of them is an outright no.</p>

