      <h2 id="heavy-metals">Do marine collagen supplements contain heavy metals?</h2>
      <p>Some do, at low levels, and arsenic is the one that shows up most. This is the most legitimate safety concern about marine collagen and almost nobody writing in India mentions it.</p>
      <p>A 2025 analysis in <em>Open Medicine</em> tested fish and jellyfish collagen supplements for toxic metals. Arsenic was the most abundant element found, at a mean of 0.59 mg/kg, followed by lead at 0.13 mg/kg. Mercury was detectable in only 12% of samples. Crucially, no sample exceeded EU regulatory limits and estimated daily intakes stayed below tolerable daily intakes, so the finding is "present and worth measuring", not "dangerous". The same paper reported significant variation between brands, and that some samples had no detectable toxic metals at all.</p>
      <p>Variation between brands is the whole point. Fish accumulate arsenic from seawater, so the raw material and the purification decide the number, and nothing on a front label tells you which. The only way to know about a specific product is a batch certificate of analysis from an independent lab. Ask for it, from us or from anyone else. A brand that holds one will send it.</p>
      <p>India does not regulate this specifically. Collagen shots are sold here as nutraceuticals under the FSSAI Health Supplements and Nutraceuticals Regulations 2016, whose guidance bars any claim to prevent, treat or cure a disease and permits only structure, function and wellbeing statements backed by accepted science. That governs what we may say. It does not require anyone to publish heavy metal testing.</p>

      <h2 id="other-ingredients">What about the other ingredients in the shot?</h2>
      <p>In a multi-ingredient product the collagen is rarely the interesting part of a safety question. Three things on <a href="/pages/glutathione-for-skin">the Collabloom panel</a> deserve a direct answer.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>250mg vitamin C, and the inconvenient finding.</strong> Vitamin C is also partly converted to oxalate. A 2019 systematic review and meta-analysis in <em>Urology Journal</em> found supplemental vitamin C associated with kidney stones in men but not women, and in its dose bands the 250 to 499mg band carried an odds ratio of 1.14, with a confidence interval of 1.00 to 1.28. Collabloom's 250mg sits in that band. Two caveats belong with it: the pattern was not dose-ordered, since 500 to 999mg and above 1,500mg showed no association and the authors could not explain why, and the large Nurses' Health and Health Professionals cohorts in the <em>American Journal of Kidney Diseases</em> in 2016 found the signal in men only at 1,000mg a day or more, at a hazard ratio of 1.19. We are not going to pretend the 250mg band result is not there. If you form stones, that is one more reason to ask your doctor.</li>
        <li><strong>45mcg biotin, which is the point of stating doses.</strong> Biotin interferes with many immunoassays and can produce a falsely low troponin, which the US FDA warned about in November 2017 and again in November 2019. The dose is what decides it: hair, skin and nail supplements can carry up to 20mg, over 650 times the roughly 30mcg daily requirement. Collabloom's 45mcg is about 1.5 times that requirement, so it is not in the range that distorts a blood test. If you are taking a separate high-dose biotin product, tell the lab.</li>
        <li><strong>250mg glutathione, and what it does not do.</strong> In a randomised controlled trial across three Indonesian teaching hospitals, published in the <em>Journal of Clinical and Aesthetic Dermatology</em> in 2021, mild side effects occurred in 9% on the supplement and 8% on placebo, which is no meaningful difference. The same trial found the skin-lightening benefit was not statistically significant, at a p-value of 0.546. We write about glutathione for tone and pigmentation, never for fairness, and that trial is one of the reasons.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The rest of the Collabloom co-factors sit at co-factor doses: 1.5mg zinc, 20mg niacinamide, 4mg vitamin E, 2mg lycopene, 1.8mg beta-carotene, 100mg hyaluronic acid, 30mg oral ceramides, 240mg Polypodium leucotomos, 50mg pine bark, 50mg resveratrol, 10mg coenzyme Q10 and 5mg water-soluble curcumin, in a 35 kcal sugar-free shot. All sit well under the levels at which any of them cause recognised problems, which is also a reminder that they support a formula rather than correct a deficiency.</p>

