      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen cause weight gain?</summary>
          <p>There is no trial evidence that it does. Reports of weight gain come from user reviews rather than studies, and the usual suspect is calories: a daily collagen shot is around 35 to 40 kcal, which is too small to explain a few kilograms. If you have gained weight on a supplement, look at the total diet and at thyroid function rather than at the collagen.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen cause acne or breakouts?</summary>
          <p>It is reported but uncommon, and no controlled trial has found a link. In clinic the likelier culprits in a flavoured shot are added sugars or dairy, neither of which is in a sugar-free stevia-sweetened product. If your skin breaks out in the first fortnight, stop for two weeks and restart: a flare that does not return was probably coincidence.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen safe for the kidneys?</summary>
          <p>In healthy kidneys there is no evidence of harm at the doses sold. The two caveats are oxalate and protein load. Collagen is rich in hydroxyproline, part of which becomes oxalate, which matters if you form calcium oxalate stones. And anyone with advanced kidney disease is usually on a restricted protein intake, so an 8 to 10 gram protein shot is a question for the treating doctor.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen if I am allergic to fish?</summary>
          <p>No, not a marine collagen product. Fish collagen is itself a registered allergen, 21 of 101 fish-allergic patients in a 2020 study carried IgE against it, and anaphylaxis after hydrolysed fish collagen has been published as a case report. Both Dr Collagen shots are marine, so neither is suitable. Hydrolysis reduces allergen content but does not reliably remove the risk.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen safe during pregnancy or breastfeeding?</summary>
          <p>Nobody knows, because it has not been tested. Trial safety data come from non-pregnant adults, so the honest answer is that there is no evidence of harm and no evidence of safety either. The separate concern is heavy metals, since arsenic and lead cross the placenta. Ask your obstetrician, and treat any brand that tells you it is definitely safe with suspicion.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen raise uric acid or trigger gout?</summary>
          <p>No trial has measured uric acid on a collagen supplement, so claims in either direction are unsupported. Collagen is low in purines compared with organ meats or shellfish, which argues against a risk. The glycine in it has been shown to increase urinary uric acid excretion, in a 2019 Nutrients study of glycine plus tryptophan, but that is not a study of collagen and does not make it a gout treatment.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long can you take collagen safely?</summary>
          <p>The longest trials ran about 12 months, so anything beyond that is use without evidence rather than use against evidence. Nothing suggests a need to cycle off. The sensible approach is to reassess at three to six months: if the outcome you started for has not moved by then, the honest conclusion is that it is not going to, and stopping costs you nothing.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

